    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 042321\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n420 PM MST Sun Oct 4 2026\n\n.UPDATE...\nUpdated Aviation\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Above normal temperatures with minimal day to day changes will \nexist through the start of the week as afternoon highs reach 97 to \n103 degrees across the lower deserts.\n\n- Breezy to locally windy conditions can be expected in the mornings \nand early afternoons across south-central and southwestern Arizona \nthrough Monday with gusts reaching 20 to 35 mph.\n\n- Temperatures across southeast California and southwest Arizona \nwarm to near record levels Wednesday and Thursday. \n\n- Dry weather with zero rain chances will persist through the week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nDry and warm conditions continue across the region with broad high \npressure positioned over the Southwest CONUS. A strong E-W pressure \ngradient remains in place across southern AZ and has resulted in \nwind gusts today up to 30-40 mph across parts of south-central AZ. \nSeveral dust channels developed and were visible on satellite across \nPinal County around midday. Magnitude of wind gusts are expected to \nwane through the afternoon and this evening. High temperatures this \nafternoon will be similar to yesterday for most areas and a couple \ndegrees warmer across south-central AZ, topping out around 97-102 \ndegrees.\n\nWeather conditions heading into Monday will once again be similar to \ntoday with breezy easterly winds, as the pressure gradient persists, \nand above normal temperatures with afternoon highs again around 97-\n102 degrees. Easterly wind gusts during the Monday are forecast to \npeak around 25-35 mph in the morning and then weaken through the \nrest of the day as the pressure gradient finally relaxes with \nlowering surface pressure to the east. By Tuesday, a shortwave \ntrough, which is currently just off the CA coast, will move eastward \ninland through NV and UT and dislodge the high pressure over the \nregion while also shifting mid-level flow from E-SE to W-NW. Despite \nthese changes aloft, there will not be any notable immediate changes \nat the surface. Temperatures Tuesday will remain nearly unchanged at \n5-10 degrees above normal with highs around 97-103 degrees across \nthe lower deserts. The surface pressure gradient will at least relax \nfurther into Tuesday. There may still be some morning easterly \nbreezes, but overall winds will be much lighter Tuesday across the \nregion. Skies will remain mostly clear through the start of the \nworkweek with no rain expected\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nBy Wednesday, a quasi-omega block is shown to develop with an \nanticyclone developing near/over the SoCal coast. Forecast \nconfidence is good that this pattern will hold through the end of \nthe workweek. This will result in temperatures remaining fairly \nsteady across south-central AZ with afternoon highs in the 95-100 \ndegree range across the lower deserts and ranging from the mid 80s \nto lower 90s across the higher terrain areas. Meanwhile temperatures \nare expected to heat up across southeast CA and southwest AZ with a \nstrengthening of the nearby anticyclone. Latest NBM forecast \ncontinues to show afternoon highs reaching the 100-106 degree range \nacross the lower deserts in SE CA and SW AZ for both Wednesday and \nThursday. These temperatures will be over 10 degrees above normal \nand near daily records in both El Centro, CA and Yuma, AZ both days. \nThese temperatures also will likely lead to Major HeatRisk for this \ntime of year, with probability of Major HeatRisk at 50-70%. As a \nresult, an Extreme Heat Watch has been issued for the Yuma area and \nImperial Valley for Wednesday and Thursday. With high pressure in \nplace dry conditions are expected to persist with no chances for \nrain, except for near the White Mtns Wednesday with a weak shortwave \nmoving through the Four Corners.\n\nThe high pressure to the west is then expected to start to weaken by \nthe end of the workweek and into the weekend with a trough moving \ninto western CONUS this upcoming weekend. However, forecast \nconfidence decreases considerably by the end of the weekend and \nstart of the following week as the ensemble spread is large with the \ntiming of the high weakening and moving out of the region and the \ntrough moving in. The ensembles also disagree on the strength of the \ntrough for this upcoming weekend. Either way temperatures are \nexpected to gradually decrease back toward normal by this upcoming \nweekend. Additionally, dry conditions look to continue through at \nleast Saturday, but this could change depending on the evolution of \ntroughing during the weekend. Right now better chances for rain look \nto arrive by the beginning of next week, with the best chances \nacross the higher terrain in eastern AZ. Stay tuned for updates.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2320Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\nUnder clear skies, the primary weather issue through Monday evening \nwill frequently gusty east winds. Confidence is moderate that some \nmeasure of gustiness will persist through the evening as decoupling \nmay never fully be accomplished overnight. As a result, LLWS \nconditions may only be reached at the most sheltered aerodromes with \nsteady wind speeds and occasional gusts through the night elsewhere.\nConfidence is very good that easterly gusts 20-25kt will redevelop \nMonday mid morning before relaxing mid afternoon. While some dust \nmay locally be lofted with these wind gusts, the limited temporal \nand areal expanse should preclude surface or slantwise visibility \nissues. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo weather issues will exist through Monday evening under clear \nskies. Confidence is good that wind directions will oscillate \nbetween a daytime E/NE and overnight N/NW with speeds occasionally \nreaching 10-12kt. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nExpect easterly winds to remain elevated around 8-15 mph with early \nmorning and afternoon gusts up to 20-35 mph, and locally higher \ngusts of 35-40 mph, today mainly across SW AZ and the eastern \ndistrict. Winds will be a touch weaker Monday and then winds will \nreturn to their more typical light diurnal tendencies on Tuesday \nwith only minor afternoon upslope gustiness. These lighter winds may \nonly last a couple days before gusty easterly gradient winds develop \nagain by Friday. Dry conditions with above normal temperatures will \ncontinue through the upcoming week. A slight increase in \ntemperatures, into the 101-106 degree range is anticipated across \nsoutheast CA and southwest AZ Wednesday and Thursday. MinRHs will \nmostly range from 15-25% across the western districts to 20-35% \nacross eastern districts with fair to good overnight recovery (35-\n60%) area-wide. No wetting rain expected through the next 7 days.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for AZZ532.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Watch from Wednesday morning through Thursday \n     evening for CAZ566-567.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Benedict\nLONG TERM...Benedict/Berislavich \nAVIATION...18\nFIRE WEATHER...Benedict/Berislavich\n",
